(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).gh300=window.HALCYON_DATA.gh300||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.gh300.full||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: Triage`, title: `Two Kinds of Agent`, weeks: `Week 3`,
  tasks: [`F3`,`F1`,`R2`,`S1`,`P2`,`D2`,`V1`],
  cast: [
    [`Tomasz Nowak`,`Leads the Kraków engineering team, eight hours ahead of Denver. Proud of his people, and quick to notice when they're treated as the B team.`],
    [`Devin Marsh`,`Engineer. Fast, talented, and always looking for the setting that makes it faster.`],
    [`Renata Vega`,`Junior engineer in her first month. Asks the questions everyone else is embarrassed to ask.`],
    [`Raj Shah`,`Priya's father. Retired machinist in Aurora, with a lathe in the garage he still uses.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `She didn't sleep after that. At 2:40 she gave up, made tea, and sat at the kitchen table with her laptop and the GitHub docs, reading the way she used to read for exams, with a pen in her hand even though there was nothing to write on.`,
    `By five she had the answer, and it was embarrassing in the useful way. Copilot had two things that people called an agent. One lived inside your editor and worked while you watched. The other lived on GitHub, and once you handed it an issue it went off and did the work somewhere else, on its own, and came back with a pull request. Kraków had used the second one and waited for the first.`,
    `She typed a reply to Tomasz and deleted it, because a paragraph at five in the morning wasn't going to fix it. <span class="said" data-who="Priya">"Can I join your 3 PM? I'll show you where it went. I didn't know either until an hour ago."</span>`,
    `His answer came back in under a minute. Kraków was eight hours ahead, and he'd been at his desk since nine their time. <span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"Yes. Bring coffee for yourself. We already had ours."</span>`,
    `At 6:30 her phone buzzed with five new photos of index cards, purple felt tip on lined white. Asha had been up too. Thirty years of nights did that to a person, and retirement hadn't undone it. Across the top card she'd written: <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"Week three. You don't need to know everything. You need to know who's on your shift."</span>`,
    `Under it, smaller, a second line. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"And who's on the next one."</span>`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Asha's Shift Notes: two kinds of agent`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Who's on your shift`,
        body: [`Asha's first card was about staffing. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"Some help works beside you. Some help works the next shift while you sleep. Don't confuse them, and don't manage them the same way."</span> Under it Priya wrote what the docs said. <b>Agent mode</b> is a mode of Copilot Chat in your editor. You give it a task in plain words. It decides which files to open, makes edits across several of them, proposes terminal commands, reads the output and the errors, and tries again until the task is done or it needs you. You're there the whole time. You see each edit, keep it or undo it, and approve the commands it wants to run. In VS Code, common read only commands run without asking by default, and anything risky waits for you. It's synchronous: it works while you watch.`,
          `The <b>Copilot coding agent</b> works on GitHub. You assign it an issue the way you'd assign a teammate, or start it from chat or from the agents page on GitHub.com. It works on its own, in a development environment that GitHub Actions provides, pushes its commits to a new branch, and opens a draft pull request. When it's done it asks you for a review. It's asynchronous: it works while you're doing something else. GitHub renamed it Copilot cloud agent in 2026, and the current docs use that name. Older material, and possibly the exam, still say coding agent, so learn both names. On Copilot Business and Enterprise, an administrator has to turn on the coding agent policy before anyone can use it, and can choose which repositories it works in.`],
        terms: [[`Agent mode`,`A Copilot Chat mode in the IDE that edits multiple files, proposes terminal commands, and iterates while the developer watches and approves.`],[`Copilot coding agent`,`An agent you assign an issue to on GitHub. It works asynchronously in its own environment and opens a pull request for review. Also called the cloud agent.`]],
        exam: `If the scenario says "in the IDE" or "while the developer watches," it's agent mode. If it says "assigned an issue," "works in the background," or "opens a pull request," it's the coding agent.` },
      { h: `Taking report`,
        body: [`The second card was the one Priya taped to her monitor. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"Night shift hands off. Day shift takes report and checks the patient. Nobody takes report and goes home."</span> The coding agent's pull request is its report. The pull request links to a session log, where you can see what it tried, what it ran, and why it changed what it changed. If you want something different, you leave a comment on the pull request that mentions <code>@copilot</code>, and it picks the work back up and pushes new commits.`,
          `GitHub builds the review into the design. The coding agent can push to only one branch: a new branch whose name starts with <code>copilot/</code>, or, when you mention <code>@copilot</code> on an existing pull request, that pull request's branch. It can't mark its pull request ready for review, and it can't approve or merge it. The developer who asked it for the work can't be the one who approves it, so someone else has to review it. By default, GitHub Actions workflows don't run on its pull request until someone with write access approves them, because the code came from an agent and nobody has read it yet. A repository can choose to let them run automatically. Its environment has a firewall that limits internet access by default. If it needs tools or dependencies to build and test your project, you preinstall them with a workflow file named <code>.github/workflows/copilot-setup-steps.yml</code>. Its work uses GitHub Actions minutes as well as your plan's usage allowance. It does best with a well scoped issue: a clear description, acceptance criteria, and pointers to the files that matter.`],
        terms: [[`Session log`,`The record of what the coding agent did while working on a task, linked from its pull request.`],[`Setup steps`,`A workflow file, copilot-setup-steps.yml, that preinstalls what the coding agent needs in its environment.`]],
        exam: `Know the guardrails: one branch only (a new copilot/ branch, or the pull request's branch when you mention @copilot on it), no approving or merging its own work, the requester can't approve, workflows wait for approval by default, and you ask for changes by mentioning @copilot in a comment.` },
      { h: `Sign for what you checked`,
        body: [`The third card was the only one in Asha's handwriting that looked rushed, as if she'd written it fast so she couldn't talk herself out of it. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"My first year. I was twenty nine, and the off going nurse was someone I wanted to be. She said the count was right, and I signed without counting. It was off by one vial. Three of us spent until four in the morning finding it in the wrong drawer. A signature means you checked. That's all it means."</span>`,
          `Agent mode asks before it runs a terminal command that isn't on an approved list. That click is your signature. In VS Code, common read only commands are approved by default and risky ones like rm still ask. You can approve a command once, for the session or workspace, or always, add your own rules in settings, or pick a permission level like Allow all that skips the prompts. Some teams extend automatic approval to harmless commands such as running tests or listing files. Anything that changes state, like deploying, deleting, migrating a database, or applying infrastructure, needs a person who reads the command first. When you aren't sure what a command does, deny it and ask Copilot to explain it. Agent mode also keeps checkpoints in VS Code, so you can roll the files back to an earlier point in the conversation, but a checkpoint can't undo what a command already did to a server.`],
        terms: [[`Approval`,`The confirmation agent mode asks for before running a terminal command. Only meaningful if someone reads the command.`]],
        exam: `The responsible answer validates before acting: read the proposed command, prefer a dry run or plan, and keep human approval for anything that changes state.` },
      { h: `Edits, sessions, and helpers`,
        body: [`The fourth card was a list. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"Small job, small team. Long shift, clean handoff. Send a runner instead of leaving the bedside."</span> Copilot Edits is how you make changes across several files from one conversation. When you already know which files are involved, you add them yourself and Copilot proposes changes only there, and you review each one before you keep it. Agent mode finds the files on its own, which is better when you don't know where the change goes. One status note. VS Code deprecated its built in Edit mode in June 2026 and points people to agent mode, or to a custom agent that behaves like Edit mode. The exam outline still names Copilot Edits, so learn what it's for: you pick the files and Copilot proposes changes only there.`,
          `Every chat session has a limited context window, and the conversation history is part of what fills it. In a long session, early decisions get crowded out and the answers drift. The fix is to start a new session for each new task, with a short summary of the decisions that still matter. Newer editors also let the main agent hand a focused job, like searching the codebase or reading a long log, to a <b>subagent</b>. The subagent works in its own context and hands back only the result, so the main session stays clean. Your editor keeps a list of sessions, and in VS Code you can hand a session off to the coding agent to finish on GitHub. Exact menus change between releases, so learn what each one is for.`],
        terms: [[`Context window`,`The limited amount of text, including the chat history, a model can take into account at once.`],[`Subagent`,`A helper agent that does a focused task in its own context and returns only the result to the main session.`]],
        exam: `Long, drifting sessions point to context. The answer is a fresh session, a tighter prompt, or a subagent, not repeating yourself louder.` },
      { h: `New tools, new doors`,
        body: [`The last card had a door drawn on it. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"Every new tool on the cart is a new way in. Know who brought it and what it can reach."</span> The Model Context Protocol, MCP, is an open standard for connecting AI agents to outside tools and data. An MCP server might let agent mode query a database, read a ticket system, or drive a browser. In VS Code you configure servers for a project in <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>. For the coding agent, a repository administrator adds them in the repository's Copilot settings, and the GitHub MCP server comes set up with access limited to that repository. On Business and Enterprise an organization policy controls whether members can use MCP servers at all. Use servers you trust, give them the narrowest access that works, and remember that a server's tools act with whatever credentials you hand it.`,
          `Priya added one more line on Wednesday, after Ken asked. Content exclusions don't reach everywhere. When she checked in October, GitHub's docs said exclusions weren't supported in agent mode in VS Code, that the coding agent had only started honoring them in a public preview, and that third party agents weren't covered. Exclusions also miss symlinks, remote filesystems, and type or symbol information the editor passes along indirectly. That list changes, so read the current one before you count on an exclusion inside agent mode or the coding agent.`],
        terms: [[`MCP`,`Model Context Protocol. An open standard that lets Copilot agents use outside tools and data through MCP servers.`]],
        exam: `MCP extends what an agent can reach. The safe answers vet the server, limit its access, and respect the organization's MCP policy.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F3`, title:`Where it went`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At seven she was on video with Kraków, a cup of coffee she didn't want in her hand. Tomasz sat at the head of a table with four of his engineers. Behind them the window was full of a gray October afternoon.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"We assigned the logging issue to Copilot at ten this morning. Then we opened our editors and waited for it to start. Nothing happened. So, is it broken, or are we?"</span>`,`Nobody at the table was smiling. They had been the first team to sign up for anything new for two years, and Priya could see they were bracing to be the team that got it wrong.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Show them it works on GitHub, not in the editor, and open its draft PR.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`She shares her screen and opens the issue. Copilot had reacted to the assignment at 10:01 their time and linked a draft pull request a minute later. It had worked for about forty minutes in its own environment, pushing commits, and just before eleven it had asked Tomasz for a review. Everybody had been watching their editors. Tomasz laughs once, short. <span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"So it finished before lunch, and we kept it waiting all afternoon."</span>`,
          why:`The coding agent works on GitHub, asynchronously, in an environment GitHub Actions provides, and reports back through a draft pull request. Agent mode is the one that works in your editor while you watch. To follow the coding agent, look at the issue, the pull request, and its session log.`},
        {t:`Have them switch to agent mode in VS Code and paste the issue text in there.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Agent mode starts on the logging change right away, in front of them. Twenty minutes in, someone notices a draft pull request from Copilot that already has the same change in it, finished hours ago.`,
          why:`Agent mode can do the work, and it does run in the editor. But the coding agent had already done it. The fix for confusion is knowing which agent you used and where it reports back.`},
        {t:`Tell them the agent's queue is slow overnight and to give it until tomorrow.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`They wait. The finished pull request sits another day with nobody looking at it, and Kraków starts to wonder if the coding agent works at all.`,
          why:`A guess cost them a day and their confidence. The agent had already finished. Find where it reports, which is the pull request.`},
        {t:`Unassign Copilot and give the logging issue back to one of their engineers.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The engineer starts the change from scratch. A draft pull request with the work already done gets closed by a cleanup bot two weeks later.`,
          why:`Pulling the work back hides the misunderstanding instead of fixing it. The coding agent works asynchronously on GitHub. Check its pull request before deciding it failed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F3`, title:`Taking report`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The pull request changed nine files. It had a tidy summary, a link that said View session, and a yellow banner Priya hadn't seen before: workflows awaiting approval.`,`Tomasz clicked Approve. GitHub didn't let him.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"I assigned it. I'm the one who knows what I wanted. Why can't I approve it?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Get a second reviewer, approve the workflow runs, and ask @copilot for fixes.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`The newest engineer on the team takes it, a little surprised to be picked. She reads the diff and finds that the new logging writes the full request body, which in staging includes test patient identifiers. She leaves a comment on the pull request: "@copilot, log the request ID only, never the body." Twenty minutes later there's a new commit. The workflows run once someone approves them, and they pass. Tomasz watches the whole thing, then leans back and takes his glasses off.`,
          why:`GitHub builds independent review into the coding agent. The developer who asked for the work can't approve it, the agent can't approve or merge its own pull request, and by default workflows wait for someone with write access to approve them. You ask for changes by mentioning @copilot in a comment.`},
        {t:`Approve the workflow runs and merge the change once the checks come back green.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The checks pass. Nobody has read the diff yet, so nobody notices that every log line now carries a full request body until Ken's scanner flags it on Thursday.`,
          why:`Approving the workflows is a real step. Green checks only show the tests passed. A human still has to review what the agent wrote before it merges.`},
        {t:`Ask an org owner to bypass the rule so Tomasz can approve his own request.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-4,health:-4},
          after:`Ken gets the request and says no in one line. He also asks why anyone wanted to approve code nobody had read.`,
          why:`The rule exists so the person who asked for the work isn't the only person who checks it. Get a second reviewer instead of working around it.`},
        {t:`Close it and have someone rewrite the logging change by hand, to be safe.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`The rewrite takes two days. It's about the same as what the agent wrote, minus the bug a reviewer would have caught in ten minutes.`,
          why:`Throwing away the work isn't caution, it's waste. Review the pull request like any teammate's, and ask for changes in a comment.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, domain:`Responsible AI`, task:`R2`, title:`The command he didn't read`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday at 11:20 the staging database came back empty. Two days of test data that Kraków had seeded for their integration tests were gone.`,`It took Priya fifteen minutes to read the session. Anders had been in agent mode, asking it to fix drift in the staging Terraform. It proposed running terraform apply with the flag that skips its own yes or no question. Anders had clicked Continue while he was on another call. If he'd run a plan first, it would have told him the database was about to be replaced.`,`Anders had been using agent mode for a week without telling anyone, which was its own kind of news.`,`She looked at her old runbook on the desk, the one with "ask Anders" in the margins in her own handwriting. Asha's rushed card was still on her phone, three photos in. A signature means you checked. She texted Asha before she could stop herself.`,`<prop type="text" from="Asha" time="11:52 AM">Who knows he didn't read it? Him, or just you?</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Talk to Anders alone, then write it up with him, no blame, and fix the rule.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:6,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`She finds him in the small room by the kitchen, laptop closed. She brings the runbook and doesn't open it. <span class="said" data-who="Priya">"Half the pages in here say ask Anders,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Priya">"So I'm asking. What happened?"</span> He turns his closed laptop a quarter turn on the table, then back. <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"I told sixty people I'd be on call for this. I didn't think I'd be the page."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"It looked like something I'd type. That's the whole problem. It looked like me."</span> They write the note together. Agent mode never runs a state changing infrastructure command without a reviewed plan, and nobody approves a command they haven't read. Anders posts it under his own name. Tomasz replies first. <span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"Thank you for saying it was you."</span>`,
          why:`An approval only counts if someone reads what they're approving. Validating AI output includes the commands it proposes, not only the code. Going to the person first and then fixing the process keeps a mistake useful instead of shameful.`},
        {t:`Write a blameless incident note herself and post the new approval rule tonight.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:2},
          after:`The rule is right and the note is fair. Anders reads it in the channel with everyone else, and for the rest of the week he's polite to her in a way he never used to be.`,
          why:`A blameless writeup and a clear rule are both good. Skipping the conversation with the person who made the mistake costs trust you'll need later.`},
        {t:`Post in the channel that Anders skipped review, so everyone learns from it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-7,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Everyone learns something. Mostly they learn that a mistake gets your name in the channel, and two people quietly stop admitting when agent mode surprises them.`,
          why:`Naming the person teaches people to hide problems. The lesson belongs in the process: read every command before you approve it.`},
        {t:`Tell the infra team to stop using agent mode on Terraform until next quarter.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:1},
          after:`The infra team goes back to typing everything by hand. The habit that caused the incident, approving without reading, doesn't get fixed. It just waits for the next tool.`,
          why:`Banning the tool skips the real control. Responsible use means a human reads and validates each command, especially anything that changes state.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F1`, title:`Approve always`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By Tuesday afternoon everyone had heard about staging. Devin had a different conclusion than everyone else.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Devin">"Hot take. Clicking Continue fifty times a day is what made him stop reading. There's a setting that approves terminal commands automatically. Turn it on and I ship twice as fast."</span>`,`He wasn't entirely wrong about the first part. Priya had seen the setting.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Turn on automatic approval for every command, since devs still check the diff.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-4,health:-6},
          after:`Devin loves it. On Friday an agent session in someone's editor runs a migration against the wrong database, and nobody was asked first.`,
          why:`The diff shows file changes. It doesn't show what a terminal command did to a server. Automatic approval for everything removes the one human check on state changing commands.`},
        {t:`Auto approve only read only commands like tests and lint, and ask for the rest.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She and Devin write the list together: run the tests, run the linter, list files, show git status. Everything else still asks. Devin grumbles that it's only half of what he wanted. A week later he admits the prompts he still gets are the ones he'd want to read anyway.`,
          why:`Agent mode asks before running commands that aren't auto approved, and you can approve once, for the session or workspace, or by rule. VS Code already auto approves common read only commands. A narrow, shared allow list for harmless commands cuts the noise that makes people stop reading. Anything that changes state keeps a human approval.`},
        {t:`Keep approval on every command, every time, and tell Devin speed isn't the goal.`, s:1, d:{trust:-2,conf:2,health:2},
          after:`It's safe. It also means fifty prompts a day, and by Thursday Priya catches herself clicking Continue on a test run without looking. Devin was right about that part.`,
          why:`Approving everything by hand is safe on paper. Too many prompts train people to stop reading, which is the failure you're trying to prevent.`},
        {t:`Leave approval settings up to each developer, since they know their setups.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Within a week half the team has turned on automatic approval for everything, and nobody can say who.`,
          why:`A safety setting left to personal taste ends up off. Set a team norm for which commands can run without a human.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F3`, title:`Four files`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday morning Renata came to Priya's desk instead of messaging. She'd been thinking about it for a while.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Renata">"I have to rename a field across the API, the model, and two tests. Four files. I know exactly which ones. Everyone says use agent mode for everything now. Is that what I should do? It feels like a lot for four files."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use agent mode and let it find every file that needs changing on its own.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`Agent mode does it. It also searches half the repository first and changes a fifth file Renata didn't mean to touch, which she undoes.`,
          why:`Agent mode works, and it's the right tool when you don't know where the change goes. When you already know the files, choosing them yourself keeps the change where you put it.`},
        {t:`Use Copilot Edits with those four files and review each change.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`She adds the four files and describes the rename. Copilot proposes a change in each one. She keeps three and stops on the fourth, where it renamed a JSON key the hospital interface reads. <span class="said" data-who="Renata">"Is that one supposed to change?"</span> It wasn't. She undoes it, and learns more about Lumen's contract with its customers in five minutes than in three weeks of reading.`,
          why:`Copilot Edits makes changes across several files from one conversation. When you pick the files, Copilot only proposes changes there, and you review each one before you keep it. Agent mode is for when you need Copilot to find the files itself.`},
        {t:`Accept inline suggestions as she types the new name in each file, one by one.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`It works, slowly. She misses a reference in one of the tests, and the build tells her an hour later.`,
          why:`Inline suggestions help as you type in one place. A change that spans files is what Copilot Edits and agent mode are for.`},
        {t:`Paste all four files into chat, then copy the answers back into each file.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The answers come back mostly right. Copying them in by hand, she drops a closing brace and spends twenty minutes finding it.`,
          why:`Copying chat answers by hand adds mistakes the editor would have avoided. Copilot Edits applies the changes in place and lets you review each one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F3`, title:`Three hours in`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday afternoon, Tomasz again, on a call with his camera off.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"We started an agent session this morning to refactor the scheduler. At ten it was brilliant. We agreed at the start to keep the old retry behavior. Now it keeps suggesting we remove it, and it brings back ideas we said no to two hours ago. It's like talking to someone who's been in the same meeting since breakfast."</span>`,`Priya thought of Asha's fourth card.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep the session going and repeat the retry rule in capital letters each time.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The capital letters work for one answer. Then the session drifts again, because every repeat makes the history longer.`,
          why:`The problem is a full context window. Adding more to the same conversation makes it worse.`},
        {t:`Switch to the biggest model available, since it can keep the most in mind.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The bigger model is better for an hour. Then the same thing happens, and the session costs more of the plan's usage allowance.`,
          why:`A larger model can help, but any context window fills up. Long, drifting sessions need a fresh start and a cleaner prompt.`},
        {t:`Start a new session with a short summary, and give side research to a subagent.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`Tomasz writes five lines: what the refactor is for, the retry rule, two ideas already rejected, and the files involved. The new session starts clean. When it needs to read four hundred lines of old scheduler logs, it hands that to a subagent and gets back three sentences. <span class="said" data-who="Tomasz">"New branch from main. Of course."</span>`,
          why:`Every session has a limited context window, and the chat history is part of what fills it. Start a new session per task with a short summary of what still matters. A subagent does a focused job in its own context and returns only the result.`},
        {t:`Start a new session per task and paste the whole old conversation into it.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The new session starts already full of the old one, rejected ideas and all. It's better for a little while.`,
          why:`A fresh session is right. Carrying the whole history over brings the clutter with it. Summarize what matters instead.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Privacy and Safeguards`, task:`S1`, title:`Ken's question`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ken caught her at the end of Wednesday, at her desk, standing, which meant it wouldn't take long and it mattered.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Ken">"The Lumen repositories are behind content exclusions. If someone assigns the coding agent an issue in one of them, or opens one in agent mode, do my exclusions hold?"</span>`,((s.flags.personalseats||G.personalseats)?`He paused. <span class="said" data-who="Ken">"And the people still on personal plans aren't behind any fence of mine at all. I know. That's a different conversation."</span>`:`Nora was copied on his calendar invite for Thursday, so whatever Priya said would end up in the evidence log.`),`Priya didn't know.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Read the exclusion limits with Ken and keep agents off those repos for now.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:5,health:5},
          after:`They read the limitations together. When they checked, the docs said agent mode in VS Code didn't honor exclusions at all, and the coding agent's support was still a public preview. Ken is quiet for a second. <span class="said" data-who="Ken">"So my fence has a gate in it."</span> They take the Lumen repositories off the coding agent's list, and Ken adds a line to the guide: no agent mode in excluded repositories until the docs say otherwise. Nora logs it with the date.`,
          why:`Content exclusions have documented limits, and the list changes. Before you count on one, read the current limitations, and close the gap with another control, like keeping the coding agent off those repositories.`},
        {t:`Tell Ken yes, since exclusions are set for the org and cover every feature.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-5,health:-5},
          after:`Ken writes it down. Two weeks later he reads the limitations himself, and comes back to ask why she told him something she hadn't checked.`,
          why:`Exclusions don't apply the same way everywhere, and the docs say so. Saying yes without checking gives a security lead a false fence.`},
        {t:`Keep the coding agent off those repos, but let agent mode run there as normal.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`It closes one gap. Ken asks about agent mode, which the docs say doesn't honor exclusions in VS Code at all, and she realizes she hasn't checked that half.`,
          why:`Turning off the coding agent for sensitive repositories is a good control. Check the documented limits for every feature you rely on, not just one.`},
        {t:`Turn off every Copilot feature in those repos and stop the rollout there for now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`The integrations team loses inline suggestions they were using safely, and two of them start pasting code into a browser chatbot instead.`,
          why:`Blanket shutdowns push people around the fence. Read the limits and close the specific gaps.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F3`, title:`Plugging in`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[((s.picks||{})[2]===2?`Thursday Anders came to her with something he'd found. He came to her anyway, which said more about him than about her.`:`Thursday Anders came to her with something he'd found, and it was the first time all week he'd sounded like himself.`),`<span class="said" data-who="Anders">"There's an MCP server on GitHub that connects agent mode to Postgres and to our ticket tracker. The agent could read the staging schema itself, look up the ticket, and stop guessing. I'll set it up for infra with my credentials this afternoon."</span>`,`His credentials were the admin ones. Priya thought about Tuesday, and then about how to say that to him.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Install it with Anders's admin credentials so the agent can fix things on its own.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-4,health:-6},
          after:`It works beautifully until an agent session decides the fastest way to fix a failing test is to change the staging schema, and it has the rights to do it.`,
          why:`An MCP server's tools act with whatever credentials you give it. Admin rights turn every tool call into a state change nobody reviewed.`},
        {t:`Use a server they've vetted, give it read only access, and check the org's MCP policy.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She asks him to read the server's code first. <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"It's two hundred lines. I can read two hundred lines."</span> He finds it sends every query to an analytics service run by someone none of them have heard of. They use the GitHub MCP server for tickets and a small server they write themselves against a read only database user. Priya checks the organization's MCP policy before anyone turns it on. Anders says it out loud: <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"I'm reading everything this week."</span>`,
          why:`MCP is an open standard that connects agents to outside tools and data. Each server is a new way in. Use servers you trust, give them the narrowest access that works, and follow the organization's policy on MCP.`},
        {t:`Use the server, but only on Anders's own laptop until the team sees how it goes.`, s:1, d:{trust:3,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`It's contained to one person. It's still the unvetted server with admin credentials, on the laptop of the person who had Tuesday.`,
          why:`Piloting with one person limits the spread. It doesn't fix an unvetted server or too much access.`},
        {t:`Ban MCP servers completely, since the agent shouldn't touch anything outside the editor.`, s:0, d:{trust:-4,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Anders shrugs and stops bringing her ideas for a while, which is a bigger loss than the server.`,
          why:`MCP is a supported way to give agents useful context. The control is vetting and least privilege, not a ban.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F3`, title:`What to hand it`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Kraków had gone from wary to hungry in three days. Thursday evening Tomasz sent her a link to a new issue.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Issue 412: Migrate auth service to the new identity provider">Move the auth service to the new identity provider. Should be straightforward.</prop>`,`It was assigned to Copilot. The auth service touched every login at every hospital that licensed Lumen.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Let it run as written, since the agent can ask questions when it gets stuck.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-5},
          after:`The coding agent doesn't stop to ask. It makes reasonable guesses about forty decisions nobody wrote down, and the pull request touches sixty files.`,
          why:`The coding agent works from what the issue says. A vague issue on a sensitive system gets a confident guess at every gap.`},
        {t:`Let it run as written, but have Tomasz watch the session log the whole time.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Tomasz watches it make the same forty guesses in real time. Watching doesn't give it the requirements.`,
          why:`The session log shows what the agent is doing. It can't make up for an issue that doesn't say what done looks like.`},
        {t:`Split it into small, clear issues, and keep the auth design itself with people.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Tomasz and two of his engineers spend an hour on the design. Then they write six small issues, each with a clear goal, acceptance criteria, and the files involved. The agent gets the four that are routine. People keep the two that decide how logins work.`,
          why:`The coding agent does best on well scoped issues with clear descriptions and acceptance criteria. Keep design decisions and the most sensitive work with people, and hand the agent the parts you can describe completely.`},
        {t:`Keep the coding agent on typo fixes until it has earned some bigger work.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Kraków stops assigning it anything. Most of what it's good at, like tests, small bugs, and docs, goes back on the pile.`,
          why:`Underusing it wastes real value. Scope the work well instead of shrinking it to nothing.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Responsible AI`, task:`R2`, title:`Green means go`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday morning Joyce stopped by with a chart. The coding agent's pull requests from Kraków were piling up in review.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Joyce">"Review is the slow part now. If the checks are green, can we just auto merge the agent's pull requests? It wrote the tests too."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Auto merge agent PRs when every check passes, and spot check a few each week.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-4,health:-6},
          after:`Merges speed up. The tests the agent wrote check what the agent thought the code should do, which isn't always what the hospital needed.`,
          why:`Green checks show the tests passed. When the same agent wrote the code and the tests, a human still has to judge whether either is right.`},
        {t:`Auto merge only the small agent PRs under fifty lines, and review the rest.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:-2},
          after:`Most changes are small. One of the small ones flips a feature flag default in the scheduler.`,
          why:`Size isn't risk. A few lines in the wrong place can matter more than a big refactor. Review every pull request.`},
        {t:`Keep a person reviewing every agent PR, same as a teammate's code.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Joyce wants something for the chart, so Priya gives her the real bottleneck: the issues were too big to review quickly. Smaller issues make smaller pull requests. By the next Friday review time is down by half, with a person on every one.`,
          why:`The coding agent can't approve or merge its own pull request, and its work gets reviewed like anyone's. Speed up review by scoping smaller issues, not by removing the reviewer.`},
        {t:`Let Copilot code review approve agent PRs, so a reviewer still signs off.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-4},
          after:`Nora points out that Copilot approvals are a preview setting someone would have to turn on, and that the agent's pull requests already get a Copilot review before anyone sees them. AI writing the code and AI signing for it isn't a second pair of eyes. The idea dies in the meeting.`,
          why:`By default, Copilot code review doesn't count toward required approvals. A preview setting can let it approve, but on agent pull requests that means AI wrote it and AI approved it. Keep a person signing off.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, domain:`Copilot Features`, task:`F1`, title:`Friday at four`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Priya had thirty minutes at the Friday engineering sync. It had been a long week of the same confusion in different clothes. Kraków had waited in their editors for an agent that lived on GitHub. Someone had asked whether agent mode opens pull requests. Devin thought Copilot CLI was just chat in a terminal window.`,`Anders, in the front row again, had his arms folded. It looked different this week.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Do one real task live in each place: inline, chat, agent mode, coding agent, CLI.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She does the inline and chat parts herself. Then she hands agent mode to Anders, who reads every command out loud before he approves it, and the coding agent to Tomasz, whose team knows it best now. Renata asks the question everyone was thinking: <span class="said" data-who="Renata">"So agent mode never opens a pull request by itself?"</span> Tomasz answers it.`,
          why:`Each surface has its own job. Inline suggestions help as you type, chat answers and explains, agent mode makes multi file changes in the editor while you approve, the coding agent works on GitHub and opens a pull request, and the CLI works in the terminal. Seeing them side by side clears up the confusion.`},
        {t:`Write a one page guide to every place Copilot runs and pin it in the channel.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`The guide is accurate, and forty people react with a thumbs up. On Monday someone in Kraków asks the same question again in a thread.`,
          why:`A written guide helps. Showing the surfaces side by side, on real work, is what makes the difference stick.`},
        {t:`Skip it. People will work out the difference after a few weeks of using it.`, s:0, flag:`agentblur`, d:{trust:-2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`She gives the slot back. People keep using the word agent for two different things, and the confusion goes underground, where it's harder to see.`,
          why:`Leaving a known confusion alone means people keep making the same mistake. Clear up agent mode versus the coding agent while it's fresh.`},
        {t:`Tell everyone to use agent mode for everything for now, to keep it simple.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Simple, and wrong for half the work. Kraków stops using the coding agent, and nobody tries the CLI.`,
          why:`One tool for everything ignores what each surface is for. Teach the difference instead of hiding it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s5`, title:`Which Copilot for the job?`, intro:`The exam loves to describe a task and ask which part of Copilot fits. Pick the best fit for each one.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`A developer is typing a function and wants Copilot to finish the line as they go.`, choices:[`Agent mode`,`Inline suggestions`,`Copilot coding agent`,`Copilot CLI`], answer:1, why:`Inline suggestions appear as you type in the editor.`},
      {prompt:`A team lead wants to assign a well scoped issue and review a pull request tomorrow morning.`, choices:[`Copilot coding agent`,`Agent mode`,`Copilot Chat`,`Inline suggestions`], answer:0, why:`The coding agent takes an issue, works asynchronously on GitHub, and opens a pull request.`},
      {prompt:`A developer wants Copilot to change several files in the editor, run the tests, and fix failures, while they watch and approve.`, choices:[`Copilot CLI`,`Inline suggestions`,`Agent mode`,`Copilot coding agent`], answer:2, why:`Agent mode makes multi file edits and proposes terminal commands in the IDE while you approve.`},
      {prompt:`An engineer working in a terminal wants help building a shell script without opening an editor.`, choices:[`Copilot coding agent`,`Copilot CLI`,`Inline suggestions`,`Agent mode`], answer:1, why:`GitHub Copilot CLI works in the terminal.`},
      {prompt:`A new engineer wants an explanation of what an unfamiliar module does before touching it.`, choices:[`Copilot coding agent`,`Copilot CLI`,`Inline suggestions`,`Copilot Chat`], answer:3, why:`Chat answers questions and explains code in the editor.`},
      {prompt:`A developer knows exactly which three files need a rename and wants to review each change before keeping it.`, choices:[`Copilot Edits`,`Copilot coding agent`,`Inline suggestions`,`Copilot CLI`], answer:0, why:`Copilot Edits makes changes across the files you choose, and you review each one.`},
      {prompt:`A maintainer wants Copilot to fix a small bug while the team is offline for the weekend.`, choices:[`Agent mode`,`Copilot Chat`,`Copilot coding agent`,`Inline suggestions`], answer:2, why:`The coding agent works asynchronously, without anyone watching, and reports back with a pull request.`},
      {prompt:`A developer asks, in the editor, why a test keeps failing, and wants an answer without any files changing.`, choices:[`Copilot Chat`,`Copilot coding agent`,`Agent mode`,`Copilot CLI`], answer:0, why:`Asking in chat gets an answer without edits. Agent mode would start changing files.`},
      {prompt:`A platform engineer wants to manage files and run a multi step task from the command line, in an interactive session.`, choices:[`Inline suggestions`,`Copilot Edits`,`Copilot coding agent`,`Copilot CLI`], answer:3, why:`Copilot CLI supports interactive sessions in the terminal, including working with files.`},
      {prompt:`A developer doesn't know which files a new feature touches and wants Copilot to find them and make the change in the editor.`, choices:[`Copilot Edits`,`Agent mode`,`Inline suggestions`,`Copilot CLI`], answer:1, why:`Agent mode finds the relevant files itself, unlike Copilot Edits, where you choose them.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`A developer assigns a GitHub issue to Copilot and then waits in VS Code for the work to begin. Nothing appears. What's the most likely explanation?`, opts:[`The editor extension needs an update`,`The coding agent works on GitHub`,`Agent mode must be turned on first`,`Issues can only go to org owners`], a:1, why:`The coding agent works asynchronously on GitHub in its own environment and reports back with a pull request. It doesn't run inside the developer's editor.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Where can a reviewer see what the Copilot coding agent tried and ran while working on an issue?`, opts:[`In the organization audit log page`,`In the developer's local editor history`,`In the session log on its pull request`,`In the repository's Insights graphs`], a:2, why:`The coding agent's pull request links to a session log that shows its steps.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`The coding agent starts work from an assigned issue. Where can it push its commits?`, opts:[`Only to a new copilot/ branch it creates`,`Directly to the repository's default branch`,`To whichever branch the issue names`,`Only to a fork it creates first`], a:0, why:`Started from an issue, the coding agent creates a new copilot/ branch and can push only to that branch, never straight to the default branch. If you mention @copilot on an existing pull request, it pushes to that pull request's branch instead.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A developer asked the coding agent to fix a bug and now tries to approve its pull request. GitHub won't allow it. Why?`, opts:[`Only org owners can approve agent work`,`The pull request is still marked draft`,`The person who asked can't approve it`,`Agent pull requests can't be approved`], a:2, why:`The developer who asked the coding agent for the work can't approve its pull request, so someone else has to review it independently.`, task:`R2`, domain:`Responsible AI`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`GitHub Actions workflows haven't run on a pull request the coding agent opened. What has to happen?`, opts:[`The agent has to rerun its session`,`Someone with write access approves them`,`The org has to buy more Actions minutes`,`The branch has to be renamed first`], a:1, why:`By default, workflows on the coding agent's pull requests wait until someone with write access approves them. A repository can choose to let them run automatically.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The coding agent keeps failing because a build tool isn't available in its environment. What's the right fix?`, opts:[`Install the tool on every developer laptop`,`Assign the issue to agent mode instead`,`Turn off the agent's firewall entirely`,`Add the tool in copilot-setup-steps.yml`], a:3, why:`A copilot-setup-steps.yml workflow preinstalls the tools and dependencies the coding agent needs.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`In agent mode, Copilot proposes a terminal command that would change a production database. What should the developer do?`, opts:[`Read it, and approve only if it's right`,`Approve it, since the diff can be undone`,`Approve it once and review the logs later`,`Let the editor decide based on settings`], a:0, why:`Agent mode asks before running any command that isn't auto approved, and a command that changes a production database should never be on that list. Approving is the human check, and it only works if the developer reads the command first. Undoing file edits doesn't undo a command.`, task:`F1`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A team wants to turn on automatic approval for every terminal command agent mode proposes. What's the main risk?`, opts:[`Suggestions will stop showing inline`,`Content exclusions will stop working`,`State changing commands run unchecked`,`The audit log will fill up too quickly`], a:2, why:`Automatic approval for everything removes the human check on commands that deploy, delete, or migrate. Teams that use it limit it to harmless commands.`, task:`R2`, domain:`Responsible AI`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A developer knows exactly which four files need changing and wants to review each proposed change. What fits best?`, opts:[`The coding agent, from an issue`,`Copilot Edits with those files`,`Inline suggestions in each file`,`Agent mode, so it finds files`], a:1, why:`Copilot Edits makes multi file changes in the files you choose, and you review each one. Agent mode is better when you don't know which files are involved.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`After three hours, an agent session keeps bringing back ideas the team rejected earlier. What's the best response?`, opts:[`Repeat the rejected ideas in each new prompt`,`Copy the full old chat into a new session`,`Keep going, since it will correct itself`,`Start fresh with a summary of decisions`], a:3, why:`Long sessions fill the context window and early decisions get crowded out. A new session with a short summary, and subagents for side tasks, keeps context clean.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`What is the Model Context Protocol (MCP)?`, opts:[`An open standard for agent tools and data`,`A GitHub policy that blocks public code matches`,`A file format for repository instruction files`,`A filter that strips secrets out of prompts`], a:0, why:`MCP is an open standard that connects AI agents to outside tools and data through MCP servers.`, task:`F3`, domain:`Copilot Features`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A security lead set content exclusions on sensitive repositories and wants to rely on them for agent features. What's the best next step?`, opts:[`Assume they apply to every Copilot feature`,`Read the documented exclusion limits`,`Turn off inline suggestions in the org`,`Ask developers to keep those files closed`], a:1, why:`Content exclusions have documented limits. Agent mode in VS Code doesn't honor them, the coding agent's support is a public preview, and symlinks and remote filesystems aren't covered. Check the current list before relying on an exclusion there.`, task:`S1`, domain:`Privacy and Safeguards`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A developer has been asking Copilot Chat about a database bug and now wants help with an unrelated CSS problem. What's the best practice?`, opts:[`Keep the same thread so Copilot has more context`,`Paste the CSS file into the database thread`,`Start a new thread for the unrelated question`,`Switch to agent mode for the CSS question`], a:2, why:`Chat history is part of the context. Starting a new thread keeps the earlier conversation from steering an unrelated answer.`, task:`P2`, domain:`Prompt Engineering`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Why can a very long chat session produce worse answers than a fresh one?`, opts:[`The model forgets part of its training hourly`,`The plan limits answers after an hour`,`Long chats turn off the content filters`,`The context window holds a limited amount`], a:3, why:`A model can only take a limited amount of text into account at once. In a long session, earlier details get crowded out.`, task:`D2`, domain:`Data and Architecture`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Which task is the best fit for the Copilot coding agent?`, opts:[`Adding unit tests to a module, with clear criteria`,`Designing the company's new identity strategy`,`Choosing which customers get a new feature first`,`Rewriting every login flow from a one line issue`], a:0, why:`The coding agent does best on well scoped work with clear acceptance criteria, like adding tests or fixing small bugs. Design and judgment calls stay with people.`, task:`V1`, domain:`Developer Productivity`, level:`AP`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    ((s.flags.agentblur||G.agentblur)
      ?`By Friday evening the channel had settled down, which wasn't the same as everyone understanding. People still said "the agent" and meant two different things. Priya heard it twice on the way to the elevator and didn't stop either time.`
      :`By Friday evening there was a whiteboard in the big room with two columns on it in somebody's square handwriting. Agent mode: in your editor, you watch, you approve. Coding agent: on GitHub, it works while you don't, you take report. Somebody had drawn a small coffee cup at the bottom of the second column.`),
    `At 5:30 Anders requested her review on a pull request, the staging Terraform fix.`,
    ((s.picks||{})[2]===3
      ?`The description had two lines. <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"I typed every line of this one myself. Read it anyway."</span>`
      :(s.picks||{})[2]===2
      ?`The description had one line. <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"Second reviewer, per the rule."</span>`
      :`The description had three lines. <span class="said" data-who="Anders">"Copilot wrote most of this. I read every line. Read it again anyway."</span>`),
    (s.score>=26&&(s.picks||{})[2]!==2
      ?`She read every line. It was good. She left one comment, on a variable called db that really meant the staging database, so he'd know she'd gone through all of it, and hit Approve. Six months ago every pull request she opened had his name in the reviewer box. She sat for a minute looking at hers in his.`
      :`She read every line and approved it. She couldn't tell if he'd asked because he trusted her or because the new rule said a second person had to look. She let it go until Monday.`),
    `On the way out she saw Devin's pull request in the feed, a cleanup of the discharge window code. Opened at 5:41, approved at 5:49, merged at 5:52. She wrote his name at the top of Monday's list and went home.`,
    `Saturday she drove out to Aurora. The garage door was up and her father was at the lathe he'd bought used when the shop closed, turning a bushing for the neighbor's mower. Asha sat on an upturned paint bucket with a paper plate of samosas. She lived four streets over and never needed much of a reason.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Raj">"This agent,"</span> he said, not looking up from the cut. <span class="said" data-who="Raj">"Works while you sleep?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Priya">"Some of it. Then somebody reads every line in the morning."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Raj">"Huh."</span> The chuck wound down. <span class="said" data-who="Raj">"So it's a second shift."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Asha">"You never answered my text,"</span> Asha said.`,
    ((s.picks||{})[2]===0
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Priya">"He knew. He was sitting in the little room with his laptop shut."</span> Asha nodded slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Asha">"Then you didn't have to tell him anything. You just had to go in."</span>`
      :(s.picks||{})[2]===3
      ?`<span class="said" data-who="Priya">"I handled it from my desk."</span> Asha didn't say anything to that. She held out the plate.`
      :`<span class="said" data-who="Priya">"I wrote it up from my desk."</span> Asha didn't say anything to that. She held out the plate.`),
    `Her father wiped his hands and gave her the setup sheet and the micrometer, same as when she was nine and stood on a milk crate to see. <span class="said" data-who="Raj">"Read me the numbers before I cut."</span>`,
    `She read them. Bore, outside diameter, length, each one to the thousandth. <span class="said" data-who="Raj">"Again,"</span> he said. She read them again, slower, and he nodded and reached for the switch.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Bug Nobody Wrote`
});
